9share (0.1.0-11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A mount block takes `access owner|any` (and `9share mount ... --access owner|any` writes it): owner, the default, lets only the mount's user use the
    mount (access=UID); any lets every user of the machine in (access=any), a decision for the administrator that is made in a file that must be root's.
    The config parser is regenerated with hbnf c699e82 (a static build made on another machine), which names the number field hyper_int and refuses
    overlong and surrogate UTF-8; the two actions of the grammar that used the old name are changed.  Tests: test_conf.c, test_mountaccess.sh.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 10:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Only the account named in `9share mount` can use a mount: the kernel mount is made with access=UID, that account's uid (it was access=any: every local
    user of the receiving machine could use the files with the exporting user's rights).  diod's own manual calls this its single-user mode.
  * A configuration file that someone else could have changed is not acted on: it must be a regular file owned by its user (the system file: by root),
    not writable by group or others, in a directory likewise.  The daemon then mounts nothing from the system file (and takes down what it had mounted),
    serve shares nothing from the user's file, the commands that edit a file refuse and change nothing, and `9share status` warns.  A chmod is noticed
    at once.  Tests: test_conf.c (every rule), test_serve.sh, test_confperm.sh.
  * `9share readahead [off|on|KB]` and a relay that looks at /etc/9share/prefetch-kb about once a second: read-ahead is switched on and off, or resized,
    on a mount that is in use, with no restart (the proxy follows the open files whether or not it reads ahead).
  * daud is brought up to diod master (164 commits since the v1.1.0 it began from; among them upstream's own fix of the shutdown hang, 16716c3).  The pages
    and unit of diod's other programs are kept in the tree, not built or installed; daud(8) no longer describes a config file and a mount helper this
    build does not have, and the usage text says daud.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 08:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * daud no longer hangs on SIGTERM when connections end at the same moment (about one run in twelve of the old shutdown test): the cleanup of a
    connection (libnpfs/conn.c, np_conn_cleanup) is uncancellable, because the shutdown cancels the connection's thread and a cancellation inside the
    cleanup left the connection uncounted, so np_srv_shutdown waited for it for ever.  New test: tests/test_daud_shutdown.c (50 connections with
    attached fids, dropped together with SIGTERM; it fails on the old daud and passes on this one).
  * libnpfs/srv.c: np_tpool_destroy sets the workers' shutdown flag and wakes them under srv->lock (a worker could miss the wakeup and sleep for ever);
    np_tpool_create is told whether its caller holds the lock.
  * test_9p now fails if daud does not leave on SIGTERM (it used to kill it after a wait and print a note).

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 06:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * msize is 128 KB (it was 1 MB): the kernel's 9p client wants a contiguous buffer of that size for each request, and a whole-file read through the
    share failed with "Cannot allocate memory" on a machine that had no free 1 MB block.  The read-ahead of 0.1.0-7 makes the speed independent of it.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The relay reads ahead, as scp does: when a file opened read-only is read in order it asks daud for the next blocks (up to 64 requests or
    2 MB: /etc/9share/prefetch-kb, 0 = off) and answers the kernel's reads from them.  src/proxy.c is the 9P-aware state machine (tags are
    mapped, flush is handled, a block is used once, writes and changes of the tree drop what was read ahead, 3 s at most); src/relay.c puts it
    between the kernel's socket and the link.  Tests: test_proxy (fake kernel and server, fuzzed under ASAN and UBSAN), test_resume (real daud,
    real link, carriers cut while reading in order: the bytes are right and the kernel's reads were answered from read-ahead).
  * Removed the hook that was to set the mount's read_ahead_kb (it never worked: the kernel names the device 9p-<n>); /etc/9share/mount-options stays.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 04:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two optional tuning files for the mount: /etc/9share/mount-options (extra kernel options, e.g. cache=readahead; 9share's own options cannot
    be overridden) and /etc/9share/readahead-kb (the mount's read_ahead_kb).  Without a cache= option every read(2) is one request answered
    before the next goes out, so a program reading 32 KB at a time got 1.3 MB/s where scp, which keeps 64 requests of 32 KB outstanding (read from
    OpenSSH's sftp-client.c), got 33 to 39 MB/s over the same ssh.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 03:00:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New commands: 9share shutdown [--force] and 9share restart (root).  They stop the daemon and wait for it (systemd: through systemctl), and
    never signal a process that is not really `9share boot`.
  * The daemon writes /run/9share.pid; `9share status` finds the daemon through it (or, for one started before there was a file, through
    /proc), so a normal user is told "running" (it said "not running" because the lock file is root-only).
  * tests: test_shutdown.sh; test_serve.sh no longer fails on a machine that really exports a directory (its last check matched any
    "--dial ... 9share link" process).

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 02:20:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Mount with msize=1048576 instead of 65536: one request carried 64 KB, so a read of a big file was one round trip per 64 KB (3.6 MB/s over a
    link with 10 ms round trip, where plain ssh does 9 to 16 MB/s).  1 MB is the author's choice; the kernel needs a contiguous block of that
    size per request, which a busy machine may not find (ENOMEM): lower it then.

 -- David Walther <david@clearbrookdistillery.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 01:50:00 -0700

9share (0.1.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 9share wsl-boot no longer writes two command lines under [boot] when the user already has one (its own line was put in at the
    [boot] header, before the user's command was seen); there is now exactly one, the script that runs the user's command first.

